/*!

\page development-mode.html
\ingroup qtappman
\title Development Mode
\brief Describes the effects of enabling the developmentMode configuration option.

\section1 Overview

The \e{Development Mode} is an optional operating mode that makes the application manager expose a
private P2P D-Bus interface, which is used exclusively by the \l{Controller}{\c appman-controller}
tool. With this interface, developers can install and manage packages, start and stop applications,
and inject intent requests from the command line without having to integrate any System UI logic.

Development mode is controlled by the \l{development-mode}{\c flags/developmentMode} configuration
option and the corresponding \c{--development-mode} command-line flag. The possible values are:

\table
\header
    \li Value
    \li Description
\row
    \li \c disabled
    \li Development mode is off. No private D-Bus interface is created and the
        \c appman-controller tool cannot connect. This is the default.
\row
    \li \c system
    \li Development mode is enabled with full, unrestricted access. Any developer
        with access to the device can use \c appman-controller to control the
        application manager without restrictions.
\row
    \li \c application
    \li A restricted development mode intended for third-party application developers.
        Access is scoped to the packages and applications whose IDs are covered by a
        developer certificate that is set at runtime.
\endtable

\section1 Enabling Development Mode

Set the mode in the \l {Configuration}{configuration file}:

\badcode
flags:
  developmentMode: system
\endcode

Or pass it on the command line:

\badcode
appman --development-mode system
\endcode

\note Development mode should never be enabled on production devices. In either \c system or
\c application mode, the private D-Bus interface can be used to install packages, start
applications, and inject intents, which bypasses normal access controls.

Starting with 6.12, enabling development mode does not relax the signature requirements for
installations going through the System UI's \l{PackageManager::startPackageInstallation} API: these
always require a valid store signature. Developer-signed packages can only be installed via the
\c appman-controller tool.

\section1 System Mode

When \c developmentMode is set to \c system, the application manager grants the
\c appman-controller tool unrestricted access to all D-Bus operations:

\list
    \li All packages and applications are visible and manageable.
    \li Developer-signed packages (without a store signature) can be installed
        from any valid developer certificate issued by the configured Developer CA.
    \li All installation tasks are visible and can be cancelled.
    \li All \c appman-controller commands are available, including commands that
        are not scoped to a specific package or application, such as taking a
        screenshot of the entire display.
    \li Intent requests can be injected for testing via \c{inject-intent-request}.
\endlist

\section1 Application Mode

When \c developmentMode is set to \c application, access through the
\c appman-controller tool is restricted to the packages and applications that are
covered by a developer certificate loaded at runtime. This mode is designed for third-party
developers who should only be able to manage their own packages on the device, without
gaining access to system packages or other developers' packages.

\section2 Setting the Developer Certificate

Before using any package- or application-specific operations in application mode,
a developer certificate must be set using \c appman-controller:

\badcode
appman-controller set-developer-certificate developer.p12
\endcode

The certificate is a PKCS#12 file that encodes what the developer is authorized to work with -
package IDs, and, as of 6.12, also application IDs, capabilities, categories and runtimes - stored
in the \c{subjectAltName} X.509v3 extension as \c{URI:qtam://<type>/<value>} entries. For details on
creating certificates, see \l{Package Installation}.

The current developer certificate persists across application manager restarts; it is saved
in the application manager's data directory. Clearing it is done with:

\badcode
appman-controller set-developer-certificate --clear
\endcode

\section2 Access Restrictions

Once a developer certificate is set, the following restrictions apply when using the
\c appman-controller tool via the development mode D-Bus interface:

\list
    \li \b{Package and application visibility}: Only packages and applications whose IDs
        match the developer certificate are listed or accessible. Packages and applications
        belonging to other developers or to the system are hidden.
    \li \b{Installation task visibility}: Only installation tasks initiated through the
        development mode D-Bus interface are visible. System-initiated tasks are not accessible.
    \li \b{Developer-signed package installation}: Installing a package that carries only a
        developer signature (without a store signature) requires the package's contents to stay
        within the bounds of the currently set developer certificate, and the developer signature
        itself must have been created with the same certificate.
    \li \b{System-wide operations}: Operations that are not scoped to a specific package or
        application, such as taking a screenshot of the entire display, are not available.
        Application-specific variants of such operations remain accessible for matching
        applications.
\endlist

\section2 Workflow Example

A typical application developer workflow using application mode:

On the development machine:
\badcode
# Sign the package with the developer's own certificate
appman-packager dev-sign-package myapp.ampkg myapp.dev-signed.ampkg developer.p12
\endcode

On the device:
\badcode
# One time only: set the developer certificate in the running application manager
appman-controller set-developer-certificate developer.p12

# Install the developer-signed package
appman-controller install-package myapp.dev-signed.ampkg

# Start the application
appman-controller start-application com.example.myapp

# Testing: Inject an intent
appman-controller inject-intent-request com.example.myapp.action '{"key": "value"}'
\endcode

\section1 Relation to Other Security Flags

Development mode interacts with the following related configuration options:

\table
\header
    \li Option
    \li Interaction
\row
    \li \l{no-security}{\c flags/noSecurity}
    \li Disables \e all security checks including signature verification. Using this flag
        makes development mode unnecessary for package installation, but is even more permissive
        and should only be used in controlled local development environments.
\row
    \li \l{allow-unsigned-packages}{\c flags/allowUnsignedPackages}
    \li Allows installation of completely unsigned packages. In application mode, this flag
        still requires a matching developer certificate when a package originates from a
        developer D-Bus client.
\endtable

\section1 Related Content

\list
  \li \l{Configuration}
  \li \l{Controller}
  \li \l{Package Installation}
  \li \l{PackageManager::developmentMode}{PackageManager.developmentMode}
\endlist
*/
